3. Networking
Virtual network: an isolated private network in the cloud (AWS VPC, OCI VCN).
Subnets: subdivisions of the network, public (internet-facing) or private (internal only).
Gateways: Internet Gateway for public access, NAT Gateway for private resources to reach out.
Routing: route tables decide where traffic goes.
Firewalls: Security Groups and NACLs (AWS), Security Lists and NSGs (OCI).
Load balancer: spreads traffic across multiple servers.
DNS: translates domain names to IPs (Route 53, OCI DNS).
CDN: caches content close to users to cut latency.
4. Cloud Security
Shared Responsibility Model
Provider is responsible for security of the cloud: data centres, hardware, core infrastructure.
Customer is responsible for security in the cloud: data, access, configuration, OS and apps (for IaaS).
Core practices
IAM: control who can do what, using users, groups, roles and policies.
Least privilege: grant only the permissions needed.
MFA: second factor on every important account.
Encryption: at rest and in transit.
Network isolation: private subnets, firewalls, restricted ports.
Logging and monitoring: CloudTrail / CloudWatch (AWS), Audit / Logging (OCI).
5. Scalability
The ability of a system to handle growing workload.
Vertical scaling (scale up): add more CPU/RAM to one machine. Simple, but has a hardware ceiling and may need downtime.
Horizontal scaling (scale out): add more machines. Better for high availability and large growth, needs a load balancer.
6. Elasticity
The ability to automatically add or remove resources as demand changes.
Scale out when traffic spikes, scale in when it drops.
Implemented with auto scaling (AWS Auto Scaling, OCI Autoscaling).
You pay only for what you use.
Scalability vs Elasticity: scalability is the capacity to grow; elasticity is growing and shrinking automatically to match demand.
